10:34:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: What people call “steam” in a shower or sauna is usually **warm, humid air**, often containing tiny suspended droplets—not a lungful of pure 100 °C water vapor. A few reasons it does not normally make you “drown”: - **Your airways already handle water vapor continuously.** Air is warmed and humidified as it travels through the nose, throat, and bronchi. By the time air reaches the deep lungs, it is normally close to body temperature (about 37 °C) and essentially fully humidified. - **There is only a limited amount of water in humid air.** Even air saturated with water vapor near shower temperature contains a relatively small mass of water per breath. If some of that vapor cools and condenses, the quantity is usually tiny—not enough to fill the lungs. - **Most deposition happens in the upper airway.** The nose, throat, and larger airways trap droplets and manage moisture with mucus, cilia, swallowing, coughing, and fluid absorption. The lungs also have mechanisms for clearing small amounts of fluid from their lining. - **The visible “steam” is often already liquid mist.** True invisible steam is water vapor. The white cloud seen above hot water is largely microscopic liquid droplets formed when vapor cools and condenses in room air. That said, **very hot steam is dangerous**. Inhaling actual high-temperature steam can cause severe burns to the airway. Because steam can condense on airway surfaces and release a lot of heat, it can cause swelling, fluid leakage, and airway obstruction. This is a burn injury—not ordinary “choking on condensed water”—and can be life-threatening. Saunas are generally tolerable because traditional saunas tend to have relatively dry air, while steam rooms use humid air at temperatures low enough that brief normal breathing is usually safe, though still uncomfortable for some people.
